// ACTOR MODEL & DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS PROBLEM BANK (Weeks 10-11)
// 320+ problems for mastery-level learning
// Each problem tests specific actor and distributed systems principles

// ============================================================================
// WEEK 10: ACTOR MODEL (180 problems)
// ============================================================================

// PRIMARY CATEGORY: ACTOR BASICS (30 problems)
// Problems 10.1.1 - 10.1.30

/*
10.1.1: Simple Echo Actor
Create an actor that receives a message and echoes it back
Actor should:
  - Implement Actor trait with receive() method
  - Store message in internal state
  - Return acknowledging response
  
Test Cases:
  - Send "hello" -> receive and store "hello"
  - Send "world" -> receive and store "world"
  - Verify isolation from other actors

Example Pattern:
struct EchoActor {
    messages: Vec<String>,
}

impl Actor for EchoActor {
    fn receive(&mut self, msg: Message) -> Result<()> {
        self.messages.push(msg.content);
        Ok(())
    }
}
*/

/*
10.1.2: Counter Actor with State
Create an actor that maintains a counter
Operations: Increment, Decrement, GetValue
Each message updates internal state
Return current value in response

Test Cases:
  - Start at 0
  - Increment 5 times -> 5
  - Decrement 3 times -> 2
  - GetValue -> 2
*/

/*
10.1.3: Actor Named Instance
Create named actors in a registry
Registry should:
  - Store actor by name
  - Support lookup by name
  - Allow sending messages to named actors
  
Test Cases:
  - Create "counter1" actor
  - Create "counter2" actor
  - Send message to counter1, verify counter2 unchanged
*/

/*
10.1.4: Actor Message Types
Define message enum with variants
Messages: AddFunds, WithdrawFunds, CheckBalance
Use pattern matching to handle each type

Test Cases:
  - AddFunds(100) -> balance becomes 100
  - WithdrawFunds(30) -> balance becomes 70
  - CheckBalance -> returns 70
*/

/*
10.1.5: Actor That Panics
Create actor that fails on certain input
Implement error handling in receive()
Return Err on invalid operations

Test Cases:
  - Valid message -> Ok(())
  - Invalid message -> Err("error message")
  - Ensure actor doesn't panic, returns error
*/

/*
10.1.6: Multiple Actors Send Messages
Create 3 actors that send to each other
Implement message forwarding
Track message flow through all 3 actors

Test Cases:
  - Actor A sends to B
  - B forwards to C
  - C returns result
  - Verify message path
*/

/*
10.1.7: Actor with Timeout
Actor should handle slow message processing
Implement timeout mechanism
Return error if processing takes too long

Test Cases:
  - Message processes in 100ms -> success
  - Message takes 5s (timeout 1s) -> error
*/

/*
10.1.8: Actor Ordering Guarantee
Send 10 messages in sequence
Verify they are processed in FIFO order
Each message should have a sequence number

Test Cases:
  - Send messages 1,2,3,4,5
  - Verify processing order is 1,2,3,4,5
  - Not 1,3,2,5,4
*/

/*
10.1.9: Actor State Isolation
Create two actors with same type
Modify one actor's state
Verify other actor's state unchanged

Test Cases:
  - Actor1 counter = 5
  - Actor2 counter = 0
  - Increment Actor1 -> Actor1=6, Actor2=0
*/

/*
10.1.10: Actor Message Queuing
Send 100 messages rapidly to one actor
All messages should be queued and processed
No messages should be dropped

Test Cases:
  - Send 100 messages
  - Verify actor processes all 100
  - Check sizes in queue decrease
*/

/*
10.1.11: Actor Self-Send
Actor sends message to itself
Implement recursive message handling
Prevent infinite loops with counter

Test Cases:
  - Actor sends to itself 5 times
  - Counter decrements each time
  - Reaches 0 and stops
*/

/*
10.1.12: Actor Kill Signal
Send special Kill message to actor
Actor should terminate gracefully
Verify no more messages processed

Test Cases:
  - Send Kill message
  - Actor stops receiving
  - Subsequent messages rejected
*/

/*
10.1.13: Get Actor Handle
Store reference to actor
Send multiple messages via same handle
Verify all messages reach same actor

Test Cases:
  - Get handle to actor
  - Send 5 messages via handle
  - Verify all 5 processed by same actor
*/

/*
10.1.14: Actor Request-Response
Actor receives request, sends response
Use correlation ID to match request-response
Handler waits for response with timeout

Test Cases:
  - Send request with ID 1
  - Receive response with ID 1
  - Timeout if no response in 5s
*/

/*
10.1.15: Actor Batching
Collect multiple messages
Process batch when threshold reached or timeout
Return aggregated result

Test Cases:
  - Add messages to batch
  - Process when batch size >= 5
  - Or timeout after 10s
*/

/*
10.1.16: Stateless Actor Pool
Create pool of identical stateless actors
Distribute messages round-robin
Verify equal distribution

Test Cases:
  - 3 actors, 30 messages
  - Each actor gets ~10 messages
*/

/*
10.1.17: Actor Composition
One actor coordinates multiple child actors
Distribute work to children
Aggregate results

Test Cases:
  - Parent sends tasks to 3 child actors
  - Each child processes task
  - Parent collects results
*/

/*
10.1.18: Actor Restart
Actor fails and is restarted
New instance takes over
State may or may not be preserved

Test Cases:
  - Actor processing
  - Actor crashes
  - Supervisor restarts actor
  - New actor continues processing
*/

/*
10.1.19: Actor Deduplication
Ensure duplicate messages not processed twice
Track processed message IDs
Reject duplicates with same ID

Test Cases:
  - Send message with ID 1
  - Send duplicate with ID 1
  - Only first is processed
*/

/*
10.1.20: Actor Sharding
Keys map to specific actors
"key1" always goes to same actor
"key2" goes to different actor

Test Cases:
  - Send key1-msg1 to shard(key1)
  - Send key1-msg2 to shard(key1)
  - Same actor receives both
*/

/*
10.1.21: Actor with Middleware
Wrap actor with logging middleware
Log all messages sent/received
Verify logs contain all messages

Test Cases:
  - Send 5 messages
  - Check logs contain all 5
  - Verify timestamps in order
*/

/*
10.1.22: Actor Backpressure
When queue is full, sender should wait
Implement queue size limit
Block sender until space available

Test Cases:
  - Queue max size 10
  - Send 20 messages
  - Sender waits when queue full
*/

/*
10.1.23: Actor Hot Swap Code
Replace actor's behavior at runtime
Old behavior -> New behavior
No message loss during swap

Test Cases:
  - Version 1: increment only
  - Swap to Version 2: increment or decrement
  - No messages lost
*/

/*
10.1.24: Actor Persistence
Save actor state to disk
Load state on restart
Verify state matches after reload

Test Cases:
  - Actor counter = 42
  - Save to disk
  - Restart actor
  - Counter still 42
*/

/*
10.1.25: Actor Clustering
Actors on different nodes
Node 1 actor sends to Node 2 actor
Use ActorRef for remote reference

Test Cases:
  - Create handle to remote actor
  - Send message via network
  - Receive response
*/

/*
10.1.26: Actor Group Broadcast
Send message to group of actors
All actors in group receive message
Collect responses from all

Test Cases:
  - 5 actors in group
  - Broadcast message
  - Receive 5 responses
*/

/*
10.1.27: Actor Deadlock Detection
Detect when actors wait for each other
A waits for B, B waits for A
Report deadlock error

Test Cases:
  - A.send(msg_to_B)
  - B.send(msg_to_A) - deadlock!
  - System detects and reports
*/

/*
10.1.28: Actor Graceful Shutdown
Stop actor and all children
Allow in-flight messages to complete
Then fully terminate

Test Cases:
  - 5 messages in queue
  - Initiate shutdown
  - Queue drains
  - Actor terminates
*/

/*
10.1.29: Actor Type Safety
Compile-time guarantee of messages
Wrong message type prevents compilation
Type system catches errors

Test Cases:
  - Actor expects Message type A
  - Compiler error on Message type B
  - Type-safe interactions
*/

/*
10.1.30: Actor Testing Framework
Create test harness for actors
Send test messages
Assert on responses
Verify actor behavior

Test Cases:
  - Create test actor
  - Send test message
  - Assert response equals expected
*/

// ============================================================================
// SUPERVISION PATTERNS (35 problems)
// Problems 10.2.1 - 10.2.35

/*
10.2.1: One-For-One Restart
Child actor crashes
Only that child is restarted
Other children unaffected

Test Cases:
  - 3 children: C1, C2, C3
  - C2 crashes
  - Only C2 restarted
  - C1, C3 continue working
*/

/*
10.2.2: All-For-One Restart
Any child crashes
All children are restarted
Entire supervision group restarts

Test Cases:
  - 3 children
  - C2 crashes
  - All 3 restarted
  - Clean slate for all
*/

/*
10.2.3: Supervisor Chain
Grandparent -> Parent -> Child
Child crashes -> Parent restarts
Parent crashes -> Grandparent restarts

Test Cases:
  - 3-level hierarchy
  - Child crashes -> Parent tries restart
  - If Parent fails -> Grandparent restarts both
*/

/*
10.2.4: Restart with BackOff
First restart immediately
Second restart after 1s
Third restart after 2s
Exponential backoff

Test Cases:
  - Crash 1 -> restart at t=0
  - Crash 2 -> restart at t=1
  - Crash 3 -> restart at t=3
  - Crash 4 -> restart at t=7
*/

/*
10.2.5: Max Restart Limit
Restart up to 5 times
After 5 crashes, give up
Propagate failure upward

Test Cases:
  - Crash 5 times -> all restarted
  - Crash 6 times -> escalate to parent
*/

/*
10.2.6: Restart Only If Error
Success -> no restart needed
Panic -> restart required
Circuit breaker preventing restart storm

Test Cases:
  - Operation succeeds -> no restart
  - Panic occurs -> restart
*/

/*
10.2.7: Delayed Escalation
Try to recover locally first
After N failures, escalate to parent
Parent decides what to do

Test Cases:
  - Child fails 3 times
  - Child handles locally
  - 4th failure -> escalate
  - Parent may restart grandparent
*/

/*
10.2.8: Supervision Group State
Track which children are failing
Identify problematic actor
Apply targeted fixes

Test Cases:
  - Monitor 10 children
  - 2 are failing repeatedly
  - Others healthy
  - Report unhealthy actors
*/

/*
10.2.9: Custom Restart Strategy
Define custom enum: RestartStrategy
Implement different strategies per child
Parent applies appropriate strategy

Test Cases:
  - Child1: Permanent (if fails, give up)
  - Child2: Transient (retry always)
  - Apply correct strategy per child
*/

/*
10.2.10: Partial Failure Isolation
Child1 fails but doesn't affect Child2
Each child is isolated
Crash in one doesn't cascade

Test Cases:
  - Child1 stores data
  - Child1 crashes
  - Child2 can still access its data
  - No corruption across children
*/

/*
10.2.11: Supervisor With Timeout
If child doesn't respond in 5s
Assume hung and restart
Timeout applies to all operations

Test Cases:
  - Send message to child
  - Child hangs
  - After 5s timeout -> restart child
*/

/*
10.2.12: Replication Factor
Keep N copies of critical actors
If one crashes, others still serve
Automatic failover

Test Cases:
  - Replicate actor 3 times
  - Instance 1 crashes
  - Instances 2, 3 continue
  - Automatically replace instance 1
*/

/*
10.2.13: Catastrophic Failure Detection
If child fails repeatedly in short time
Escalate to parent
Parent decides: restart or kill

Test Cases:
  - Child fails 5 times in 10s
  - Escalate to parent
  - Parent: shut down child permanently
*/

/*
10.2.14: Graceful Degradation
Some children crash
System keeps running in degraded mode
Partial service available

Test Cases:
  - 4 service instances
  - 2 crash
  - System provides 50% capacity
  - Keep serving with what's left
*/

/*
10.2.15: Health Check
Supervisor periodically checks child health
Send ping, wait for pong
If no pong, treat as dead

Test Cases:
  - Ping child every 1s
  - Normal child responds
  - Dead child doesn't respond -> restart
*/

/*
10.2.16: Restart Notification
Child receives notification before restart
Cleanup resources
Prepare for shutdown

Test Cases:
  - Before restart, send "prepare_shutdown"
  - Actor flushes buffers
  - Closes connections
  - Then restart
*/

/*
10.2.17: Supervisor Hierarchy Limits
Don't create too deep hierarchies
Max depth 5 levels
Pre-allocate supervisor pool

Test Cases:
  - Create 5-level hierarchy -> OK
  - Try 6 levels -> error
*/

/*
10.2.18: Load-based Restart
Restart when load too high
If CPU > 80%, restart to reset state
Fast recovery under overload

Test Cases:
  - CPU usage 50% -> no restart
  - CPU usage 90% -> restart
  - CPU drops to 30% after restart
*/

/*
10.2.19: Dependency-based Restart
Child depends on external service
If service unavailable, don't restart child
Wait for service, then start child

Test Cases:
  - Service1 required by Child
  - Service1 unavailable
  - Don't restart Child yet
  - Once Service1 available, start Child
*/

/*
10.2.20: Canary Restart
Restart new version on subset of children
Monitor success rate
If good, roll out to all

Test Cases:
  - New version V2
  - Restart 1 of 10 children with V2
  - Monitor: success rate 99%
  - Roll out V2 to remaining 9
*/

/*
10.2.21: Supervisor Metrics
Track restart counts per child
Identify problematic actors
Report in monitoring dashboard

Test Cases:
  - Child1: 5 restarts
  - Child2: 50 restarts (problem!)
  - Child3: 0 restarts
  - Fix Child2
*/

/*
10.2.22: Exponential Backoff with Jitter
Restart backoff: 100ms, 200ms, 400ms, 800ms
Add random jitter: +/-10%
Prevent thundering herd

Test Cases:
  - Multiple children crash simultaneously
  - With jitter, restarts spread out
  - Not all restarting at exact same time
*/

/*
10.2.23: Idempotent Restart
Restarting twice = restarting once
No side effects from duplicate restart
Safe to retry restart

Test Cases:
  - Send restart(actor1) twice
  - Same result as sending once
  - No double-initialization
*/

/*
10.2.24: Supervised Pool Scaling
Supervisor monitors pool size
If child dies, spawn replacement
Maintain constant pool size

Test Cases:
  - Pool size 10
  - Child dies
  - Automatically spawn 1 new child
  - Pool size back to 10
*/

/*
10.2.25: Supervisor Linking
Parent-child link: if parent dies, kill child
Child-parent link: if child dies, kill parent
Bidirectional dependency

Test Cases:
  - Parent and Child linked
  - Parent dies -> kill Child
  - Child dies -> kill Parent
*/

/*
10.2.26: Nanny Pattern
Special actor that restarts children
Separate from normal actor tree
Focused on recovery

Test Cases:
  - Nanny monitors 5 critical actors
  - Any dies -> Nanny restarts
  - Nanny itself is restarted by supervisor
*/

/*
10.2.27: Exponential Queue Backoff
Queue size increases -> increase backoff
Deep queue means system overloaded
Increase restart delay

Test Cases:
  - Queue size < 10 -> restart fast
  - Queue size > 100 -> restart slow
  - Queue size > 1000 -> very slow restart
*/

/*
10.2.28: Transient vs Permanent Errors
Transient: network timeout -> retry
Permanent: invalid message -> give up
kmeth each appropriately

Test Cases:
  - Network error (transient) -> restart
  - Code error (permanent) -> escalate
*/

/*
10.2.29: Supervised Timeout
Child must complete within time limit
Timeout persists across restarts
Ensures no stuck actors

Test Cases:
  - Task must finish in 5s
  - Restart happens, timer resets
  - But overall deadline remains
*/

/*
10.2.30: Hierarchical Supervision Report
Print tree of supervisors and children
Show restart counts per node
Visualize supervision structure

Test Cases:
  - Print supervision tree
  - Show metrics at each level
  - Identify problem areas
*/

/*
10.2.31: Restart with State Recovery
Actor fails, restart with last known good state
State saved before crashes
Restores to safe point

Test Cases:
  - Save state every 10 messages
  - Crash after 15 messages
  - Restart with state from 10 messages
  - Skip corrupted messages 11-15
*/

/*
10.2.32: Supervision Annotation
Mark critical actors: @Critical
Mark optional actors: @Optional
Restart strategy depends on annotation

Test Cases:
  - Critical actor fails -> aggressive restart
  - Optional actor fails -> may skip restart
*/

/*
10.2.33: Batch Restart
If N children fail simultaneously
Restart all at once with same delay
Avoid sequential restart storms

Test Cases:
  - 5 children crash at t=0
  - Restart all 5 at t=1 (together)
  - Not spread over 5s
*/

/*
10.2.34: Supervisor Recursion Limit
Prevent infinite restart loops
If restart threshold exceeded
Escalate to parent and stop

Test Cases:
  - Max 5 restarts per minute
  - Exceed -> escalate
  - Stop attempting restart
*/

/*
10.2.35: Dynamic Supervisor Addition
Add new child to supervisor at runtime
Supervisor adoption of migrated actors
Hot addition without stop

Test Cases:
  - Running supervisor with 2 children
  - Add 3rd child while running
  - All 3 supervised immediately
*/

// ============================================================================
// SERVICE ARCHITECTURE (45 problems)
// Problems 10.3.1 - 10.3.45

/*
10.3.1: Request-Response Pattern
Send request, wait for response
Correlation ID matches request-response
Timeout if no response

Test Cases:
  - Send request(id=1)
  - Receive response(id=1)
  - Verify correlation
*/

/*
10.3.2: Fire-and-Forget Pattern
Send message, don't wait for response
Used for notifications, logs
Async one-way communication

Test Cases:
  - Send message
  - Don't wait for ack
  - Caller returns immediately
*/

/*
10.3.3: Ask Pattern
Request with automatic response handler
await response_future.await
Cleaner syntax than manual correlation

Test Cases:
  - ask(actor, message).await
  - Returns Response directly
  - Handles correlation internally
*/

/*
10.3.4: Tell Pattern
Send message, no response expected
actor.tell(message)
Pure async notification

Test Cases:
  - actor.tell(Update)
  - Continue immediately
  - Don't await
*/

/*
10.3.5: Service Discovery
Lookup actor by name/role
Returns ActorRef if found
Returns error if not found

Test Cases:
  - discover("database") -> Some(ActorRef)
  - discover("missing") -> None
*/

/*
10.3.6: Service Registry
Register: name -> ActorRef
Lookup: name -> ActorRef
Deregister: remove from registry

Test Cases:
  - register("cache", cache_ref)
  - lookup("cache") -> Some(cache_ref)
  - deregister("cache")
  - lookup("cache") -> None
*/

/*
10.3.7: Load Balancer
Route messages to least busy replica
Track queue depth per instance
Send to instance with smallest queue

Test Cases:
  - Instance1 queue: 5
  - Instance2 queue: 2
  - Send to Instance2 (smaller queue)
*/

/*
10.3.8: Round Robin Distribution
Cycle through instances 1,2,3,1,2,3
Each gets equal load
Simple but fair

Test Cases:
  - Send 6 messages
  - Instance1 gets 1,4
  - Instance2 gets 2,5
  - Instance3 gets 3,6
*/

/*
10.3.9: Sticky Sessions
Client always goes to same server
Based on client ID
Maintains affinity

Test Cases:
  - Client=A -> always to Server1
  - Client=B -> always to Server2
  - Multiple requests from same client
*/

/*
10.3.10: Consistent Hashing
Map key to specific instance
Same key always same instance
Minimize redistribution on node change

Test Cases:
  - key="user1" -> Instance2
  - key="user1" again -> Instance2 (consistent)
  - key="user2" -> Instance4
*/

/*
10.3.11: Replication Factor
Keep N copies (default N=3)
Read from any copy
Write to all copies

Test Cases:
  - 3 replicas of data
  - Write to all 3
  - Read from 1 (all have same data)
*/

/*
10.3.12: Write Quorum
Write to majority (N/2 + 1)
Read from majority
Ensures consistency if one fails

Test Cases:
  - 3 replicas
  - Write to 2 of 3 (quorum)
  - Read from 2 of 3 (quorum)
  - Always get latest data
*/

/*
10.3.13: Read-Through Cache
Check cache first
If miss, query database
Update cache with result
Return to client

Test Cases:
  - First request: miss, hit DB, cache updated
  - Second request: cache hit, fast
*/

/*
10.3.14: Write-Through Cache
Update cache and database
Ensure consistency
Single write operation

Test Cases:
  - Write X -> update cache and DB
  - Both have X
  - Consistency guaranteed
*/

/*
10.3.15: Batch Processing
Collect messages, process batch
Wait for size threshold or timeout
More efficient than one-by-one

Test Cases:
  - Batch size = 10
  - After 10 messages -> process batch
  - Timeout 1s also triggers batch
*/

/*
10.3.16: Rate Limiting
Allow max N requests per second
Reject excess requests
Backpressure on clients

Test Cases:
  - Limit 100 req/s
  - 100 requests -> OK
  - 101st request -> rate limited
*/

/*
10.3.17: Circuit Breaker
Count failures in window
If threshold exceeded, open circuit
Fail fast without calling service

Test Cases:
  - Service fails 5 times in 10s
  - Circuit opens
  - New requests fail immediately (fast)
*/

/*
10.3.18: Bulkhead Pattern
Isolate thread pools per service
One service overload doesn't affect others
Prevent cascade failures

Test Cases:
  - ServiceA pool: 5 threads
  - ServiceB pool: 5 threads
  - ServiceA overloaded
  - ServiceB still responsive
*/

/*
10.3.19: Timeout Handler
Set timeout on requests
If no response in N seconds
Return error or fallback

Test Cases:
  - Request with 5s timeout
  - Response after 3s -> OK
  - No response after 5s -> timeout error
*/

/*
10.3.20: Retry Logic
Retry failed request N times
Exponential backoff between retries
Give up after N failures

Test Cases:
  - Fail, retry after 1s
  - Fail, retry after 2s
  - Fail, retry after 4s
  - Give up after 3 retries
*/

/*
10.3.21: Fallback Handler
Primary fails -> use fallback
Fallback is simpler/slower alternative
Better degraded service than failure

Test Cases:
  - Primary cache fails
  - Fallback to slow DB query
  - Return result instead of error
*/

/*
10.3.22: Decoupling with Queue
Producer adds to queue
Consumer pulls from queue
Producer and consumer independent

Test Cases:
  - Producer generates 100 messages
  - Consumer processes 10/s
  - No blocking between producer-consumer
*/

/*
10.3.23: Message Ordering
Producer: 1,2,3,4,5
Consumer: guaranteed to process 1,2,3,4,5
FIFO ordering preserved

Test Cases:
  - Send messages 1-100 in order
  - Process received in same order
  - No reordering
*/

/*
10.3.24: Message Deduplication
Detect duplicate messages
Only process once
Track processed message IDs

Test Cases:
  - Receive message ID=5
  - Receive duplicate ID=5
  - Process once
  - Ignore duplicate
*/

/*
10.3.25: Idempotent Operation
Operation safe to repeat multiple times
Result same whether done once or 5 times
kmeth retries safely

Test Cases:
  - Transfer $100 once -> balance = 500
  - Retry transfer -> balance = 500 (not 400)
*/

/*
10.3.26: Transaction Scope
Group operations: all-or-nothing
Commit if all succeed
Rollback if any fail

Test Cases:
  - Transfer: debit A, credit B
  - Debit succeeds, credit fails
  - Rollback debit
  - Both fail or both succeed
*/

/*
10.3.27: Compensating Transaction
Forward transaction fails
Apply compensating transaction (undo)
Return to previous state

Test Cases:
  - Transfer: debit A, credit B
  - Credit fails
  - Run compensating: credit A back
*/

/*
10.3.28: Message Expiration
Message expires if not processed in N seconds
Producer sets TTL
Consumer drops expired messages

Test Cases:
  - Message TTL = 5s
  - After 5s, message discarded
  - Before 5s, still valid
*/

/*
10.3.29: Priority Queue
High priority messages processed first
Low priority when queue not full
Starvation prevention with aging

Test Cases:
  - Priority: High, Medium, Low
  - Despite order sent: High processed first
*/

/*
10.3.30: Persistence
Store messages to disk before processing
Survive crashes
Replay on recovery

Test Cases:
  - Message arrives
  - Write to disk
  - Process
  - If crash, replay from disk
*/

/*
10.3.31: Compaction
Old duplicate messages can be removed
Keep only latest version
Reduce storage

Test Cases:
  - KeyX version 1 (old)
  - KeyX version 2 (newer)
  - Compact: remove version 1
*/

/*
10.3.32: Snapshot
Save actor state periodically
Snapshot every 1000 messages
Speed up recovery from old state

Test Cases:
  - Snapshot at message 1000
  - Crash at message 1500
  - Recover from snapshot (msg 1000)
  - Replay only 500 messages
*/

/*
10.3.33: Event Sourcing
Store events, not state
Rebuild state from events
Full audit trail

Test Cases:
  - Events: deposit(100), withdraw(30)
  - State: balance = 70
  - Derive state from events
*/

/*
10.3.34: CQRS Pattern
Command: change state
Query: read state
Separate read and write paths

Test Cases:
  - Command: deposit(100)
  - Query: getBalance() -> 100
  - Different handlers
*/

/*
10.3.35: Saga Pattern
Long-running transaction across multiple services
Service1.step1 -> Service2.step2 -> Service3.step3
Compensate each step on failure

Test Cases:
  - Order saga: validate order -> debit account -> ship items
  - Ship fails -> refund account -> cancel order
*/

/*
10.3.36: Distributed Lock
Lock shared resource
Only one actor holds lock
Others wait for availability

Test Cases:
  - Actor1 locks resource
  - Actor2 requests -> waits
  - Actor1 unlocks
  - Actor2 gets lock
*/

/*
10.3.37: Lease-based Lock
Lock expires after lease period
No explicit unlock needed
Auto-expires to prevent deadlock

Test Cases:
  - Lock with 30s lease
  - If holder dies, lock expires
  - Others can acquire after expiry
*/

/*
10.3.38: Optimistic Locking
No lock, check version
If version matches, update
If version changed, retry

Test Cases:
  - Read X version=1
  - Update if version still 1
  - Concurrent update changes version
  - Retry needed
*/

/*
10.3.39: Pessimistic Locking
Lock before reading
Hold lock through operation
Release when done

Test Cases:
  - Lock before read
  - Read X
  - Update X
  - Release lock
*/

/*
10.3.40: Pipeline
Chain operations: filter -> map -> reduce
Each stage can be parallelized
Improve throughput with buffering

Test Cases:
  - Stage1: parse (100/s)
  - Stage2: validate (200/s)
  - Stage3: save (50/s)
  - Overall: 50/s (limited by save)
*/

/*
10.3.41: Fan-Out/Fan-In
Send to multiple handlers (fan-out)
Collect responses (fan-in)
Parallelize independent work

Test Cases:
  - Send to 3 handlers
  - Collect 3 results
  - Return when all complete
*/

/*
10.3.42: Scatter-Gather
Scatter: send to all nodes
Gather: collect responses
Return aggregated result

Test Cases:
  - Query to 5 database nodes
  - Nodes return partial results
  - Aggregate into final result
*/

/*
10.3.43: Backpressure Propagation
If consumer slow, tell producer to slow down
Prevents buffer overflow
Cascades through chain

Test Cases:
  - Producer writes fast
  - Consumer slow to process
  - Buffer fills
  - Producer slows down
*/

/*
10.3.44: Graceful Drain
Stop accepting new messages
Process all queued messages
Then shutdown

Test Cases:
  - Receive 100 messages
  - Call drain()
  - Stop accepting new messages
  - Process all 100
  - Shutdown when done
*/

/*
10.3.45: Service Composition
Combine multiple services
Request flows through chain
Each service adds value

Test Cases:
  - Auth service -> Business logic -> Logging
  - Request flows through all 3
  - Response aggregates all 3 results
*/

