// WEEK 11: DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS (140 problems)
// ============================================================================

// PRIMARY CATEGORY: NETWORK & RPC (35 problems)
// Problems 11.1.1 - 11.1.35

/*
11.1.1: Simple RPC Call
Client sends request over network
Server processes
Server sends response back
Verify request/response match

Test Cases:
  - Request: add(2, 3)
  - Response: 5
  - Over network
*/

/*
11.1.2: RPC with Timeout
Call with 5 second timeout
If no response in 5s
Return error to client

Test Cases:
  - Server responds in 2s -> OK
  - Server hung -> timeout after 5s
  - Return error, not hang forever
*/

/*
11.1.3: RPC with Retries
Failed call auto-retries 3 times
Exponential backoff between retries
Give up after 3 failures

Test Cases:
  - Fail 1, retry after 1s
  - Fail 2, retry after 2s
  - Fail 3, return error
  - Success on retry 1 -> done
*/

/*
11.1.4: RPC Serialization
Serialize call: struct to bytes
Send over network
Deserialize on server
Ensure no data loss

Test Cases:
  - Struct { x: 10, y: "hello" }
  - Serialize -> bytes
  - Deserialize -> original struct
*/

/*
11.1.5: RPC with Compression
Large message compressed before send
Decompressed on receive
Reduce bandwidth

Test Cases:
  - Message 1MB
  - Compress -> 100KB
  - Decompress -> 1MB
  - Same content
*/

/*
11.1.6: RPC Connection Pooling
Keep N persistent connections
Reuse connections
Reduce overhead

Test Cases:
  - Pool size 10
  - 100 requests use same 10 connections
  - Not create 100 new connections
*/

/*
11.1.7: RPC Batching
Batch multiple RPC calls
Send as one batch message
Reduce round-trips

Test Cases:
  - 100 small calls
  - Batch into 1 message
  - 1 round-trip instead of 100
*/

/*
11.1.8: RPC Error Handling
Distinguish error types
Network error vs Application error
kmeth appropriately

Test Cases:
  - Network timeout -> retry
  - Invalid argument -> return error immediately
  - Internal server error -> retry with backoff
*/

/*
11.1.9: RPC Authentication
Include auth token in request
Server verifies token
Reject unauthorized requests

Test Cases:
  - Request with valid token -> accepted
  - Request with invalid token -> 401
  - Request without token -> 401
*/

/*
11.1.10: RPC Encryption
Encrypt messages before sending
Decrypt on receive
Secure communication

Test Cases:
  - Message: "secret"
  - Encrypt -> "a7f2k9s..."
  - Decrypt -> "secret"
*/

/*
11.1.11: RPC Rate Limiting
Track requests per client
Limit to N requests/second
Return 429 if exceeded

Test Cases:
  - Limit 100 req/s
  - Send 150 req/s
  - First 100 billed as OK
  - Last 50 rejected with 429
*/

/*
11.1.12: RPC Correlation ID
Assign unique ID per request
Pass through entire stack
Track in logs

Test Cases:
  - Request gets ID: abc123
  - All logs mention abc123
  - Can trace request from client to server
*/

/*
11.1.13: RPC Health Check
Periodic ping to server
Returns pong if healthy
Server marked down if no pong

Test Cases:
  - Ping every 10s
  - Healthy server responds
  - Dead server doesn't -> marked down
*/

/*
11.1.14: RPC Circuit Breaker
Track failures per server
If failure rate > threshold
Open circuit, fail fast
Half-open to test recovery

Test Cases:
  - 5 failures per 100 requests
  - Open circuit after 5% failure
  - Fail fast without trying server
*/

/*
11.1.15: RPC Request Deduplication
Detect duplicate requests by ID
Only execute once
Return cached response for duplicate

Test Cases:
  - Request ID=5 -> execute
  - Request ID=5 again -> return cached
  - No double execution
*/

/*
11.1.16: RPC Idempotency
Mark request as idempotent
Safe to execute multiple times
kmeth retries correctly

Test Cases:
  - Transfer with ID=1
  - Retry with same ID
  - Balance changes once, not twice
*/

/*
11.1.17: RPC One-Way Call
Send request, don't wait for response
Fire and forget
Async

Test Cases:
  - Send log message
  - Don't await response
  - Return immediately
*/

/*
11.1.18: RPC Two-Way Call
Send request, wait for response
Synchronous
Block until response received

Test Cases:
  - await rpc.call(request)
  - Block until response
  - Could timeout
*/

/*
11.1.19: RPC with Streaming
Send/receive stream of messages
Bidirectional communication
Not just request-response

Test Cases:
  - Client sends 100 chunks
  - Server receives in stream
  - Server responds with 100 chunks
*/

/*
11.1.20: RPC Version Compatibility
Support multiple RPC versions
V1 and V2 servers co-exist
Client compatible with both

Test Cases:
  - Client V2.0 calls server V1.5
  - Server V1.5 calls client V2.0
  - Both work (backward compatible)
*/

/*
11.1.21: RPC Method Dispatch
Multiple methods per service
Route to correct method
Type-safe method invocation

Test Cases:
  - Service.add(2, 3)
  - Service.subtract(5, 3)
  - Correct method called
*/

/*
11.1.22: RPC Response Marshaling
Convert response to wire format
kmeth different types
Ensure faithful representation

Test Cases:
  - Response: { x: 1.5, y: "test", z: null }
  - Marshal to JSON/binary
  - Unmarshal: same values
*/

/*
11.1.23: RPC Caching
Cache responses
Subsequent same request -> cached response
Reduce server load

Test Cases:
  - Request A -> server computes
  - Request A again -> from cache
  - Different request B -> server computes
*/

/*
11.1.24: RPC Invalidation
Update operation invalidates cache
Related queries must recompute
Consistency guaranteed

Test Cases:
  - Cache: getUser(5) = { name: "alice" }
  - Update: setName(5, "bob")
  - Cache invalidated
  - Next getUser(5) recomputes
*/

/*
11.1.25: RPC Multicast
One request, multiple servers receive
All process independently
Broadcast async

Test Cases:
  - Request to all nodes
  - All broadcast listeners get message
  - Process independently
*/

/*
11.1.26: RPC Unicast
One request, one specific server
Directed communication
Not broadcast

Test Cases:
  - Send to node 3 specifically
  - Node 1, 2 don't receive
  - Only node 3 receives
*/

/*
11.1.27: RPC Anycast
Request to closest replica
Reduce latency
Improve perceived performance

Test Cases:
  - 3 replicas: US, EU, AP
  - Client in EU -> EU replica responds
  - Reduce round-trip time
*/

/*
11.1.28: RPC Ordered Delivery
Messages delivered in sending order
FIFO guarantee per sender
Maintain causality

Test Cases:
  - Send 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
  - Receive 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
  - Not 1, 3, 2, 5, 4
*/

/*
11.1.29: RPC Ordered Semantics
Strong ordering: total order
Causal ordering: respect causality
Weak ordering: no guarantees

Test Cases:
  - Strong: all nodes see same order
  - Causal: respects dependencies
  - Weak: may see different orders
*/

/*
11.1.30: RPC Suppression
Acknowledge received but not processed
Send ACK to unblock sender
Process asynchronously

Test Cases:
  - Client sends request
  - Server: "got it" ACK immediately
  - Server processes in background
*/

/*
11.1.31: RPC Pipelining
Send multiple requests without waiting
Server responds to all
Use network efficiently

Test Cases:
  - Send 100 requests immediately
  - Receive 100 responses streaming
  - Not serial request-response
*/

/*
11.1.32: RPC Back Pressure
Slow consumer tells producer to slow down
Prevent buffer overflow
Flow control

Test Cases:
  - Producer: 1000 msg/s
  - Consumer: 100 msg/s
  - Consumer tells producer to slow down
  - Producer limited to 100 msg/s
*/

/*
11.1.33: RPC Priority
High priority requests served first
Skip low priority if server busy
Quality of service differentiation

Test Cases:
  - High priority: answer immediately
  - Low priority: answer when not busy
*/

/*
11.1.34: RPC Jitter
Add randomness to retry timing
Prevent thundering herd
Smooth load spikes

Test Cases:
  - Retry delay: 1s +/- 100ms random
  - Not all clients retry at exact 1s
  - Spread out retries
*/

/*
11.1.35: RPC Tracing
Record RPC path through system
Service1 calls Service2 calls Service3
Show in request trace

Test Cases:
  - Request ID: abc123
  - Trace shows: Client -> Service1 -> Service2 -> Service3
  - All timings visible
*/

// ============================================================================
// REPLICATION & CONSISTENCY (35 problems)
// Problems 11.2.1 - 11.2.35

/*
11.2.1: Primary-Backup Replication
One primary, one backup
Writes go to primary
Primary replicates to backup
Read from primary or backup

Test Cases:
  - Write to primary -> replicate to backup
  - Primary dies -> promote backup to primary
  - Consistent data between them
*/

/*
11.2.2: Multi-Master Replication
Multiple primaries
Any can accept writes
All replicate to each other
Conflict resolution needed

Test Cases:
  - Node1 writes X
  - Node2 writes Y
  - Both have X and Y
  - Consistent eventually
*/

/*
11.2.3: Replication Lag
Backup lags behind primary
Backup = primary - delta
Eventually catches up

Test Cases:
  - Primary has 100 messages
  - Backup has 95 messages
  - Lag = 5 messages
*/

/*
11.2.4: Read Your Writes
After write, read sees same write
Client stickiness to node
Guarantee for that client

Test Cases:
  - Write X at t0
  - Read X at t1 > t0
  - See X that was just written
*/

/*
11.2.5: Causal Consistency
If B depends on A, all see A before B
Write causality respected
Partial order

Test Cases:
  - A: "create user"
  - B: "give permission to user"
  - All observers see A before B
*/

/*
11.2.6: Eventual Consistency
No guarantee when consistency reached
Will converge eventually
Temporary divergence allowed

Test Cases:
  - Nodes may differ temporarily
  - After network heals, converge
  - No guarantee on timing
*/

/*
11.2.7: Strong Consistency
All nodes same state always
Wait for replication acknowledgment
Slower but safer

Test Cases:
  - Write only returns after all replicas ack
  - Read always sees latest write
  - Slow (have to wait for all)
*/

/*
11.2.8: Quorum Reads
Read from N/2 + 1 replicas
Get latest value among them
Consistency without writing all

Test Cases:
  - 5 replicas
  - Read quorum: 3
  - Get latest from 3 of 5
*/

/*
11.2.9: Quorum Writes
Write to N/2 + 1 replicas
Ensure future reads see write
Consistency without writing all

Test Cases:
  - 5 replicas
  - Write quorum: 3
  - Write to 3 of 5 is enough
*/

/*
11.2.10: Quorum Overlap
Read quorum and write quorum overlap
Guarantees latest data read
At least one server has latest

Test Cases:
  - 5 replicas, quorum = 3
  - Write quorum and read quorum overlap
  - Always read latest
*/

/*
11.2.11: Replication Factor
Replicate data N times
Survive N-1 failures
Common: N=3

Test Cases:
  - Replication factor = 3
  - 2 node failures -> still has 1 copy
  - 3 node failures -> data lost
*/

/*
11.2.12: Replica Reconciliation
Compare replicas, sync differences
Hash-based or log-based
Repair divergences

Test Cases:
  - Node1 has data A, B, C
  - Node2 has data A, B, D
  - Reconcile: both end up with A, B, C, D
*/

/*
11.2.13: Vector Clocks
Track causality with version vectors
Detect concurrent writes
Identify which writes are related

Test Cases:
  - Write1: [1, 0]
  - Write2: [0, 1]
  - Concurrent (no causality)
  - Write3: [1, 1]
  - Write3 depends on both
*/

/*
11.2.14: Conflict Resolution
Concurrent writes to same field
Merge them using strategy
Last-write-wins, custom merge, etc.

Test Cases:
  - Node1: X = 5
  - Node2: X = 3
  - Last write wins: X = 5 (if ts > 3)
  - Or merge: X = "5,3"
*/

/*
11.2.15: Version Vectors in Replication
Each replica has version vector
Track which writes each has
Detect out-of-date replicas

Test Cases:
  - Replica1: [3, 2, 1]
  - Replica2: [3, 2, 0]
  - Replica2 missing one write from node3
  - Replica1 detects and syncs
*/

/*
11.2.16: CRDT (Conflict-Free Replicated Data Type)
Data structure that merges automatically
No explicit conflict resolution needed
Mathematically sound merge

Test Cases:
  - Counter CRDT: increment on any node
  - Merge: sum increments = total
  - No conflicts
*/

/*
11.2.17: Last-Write-Wins Register
Timestamp each write
Highest timestamp wins
Simple but can lose data

Test Cases:
  - Write1: 10 at t=0
  - Write2: 20 at t=1
  - Read: 20 (latest timestamp)
*/

/*
11.2.18: Multi-Value Register
Return multiple values if concurrent
Client must resolve
Don't lose any writes

Test Cases:
  - Write1: 10 at node1
  - Write2: 20 at node2
  - Before merge, read both
  - [10, 20] (conflict)
*/

/*
11.2.19: Tombstones
Mark deleted items with tombstone
Prevent resurrection after delete/replicate/merge
Garbage collection eventually

Test Cases:
  - Delete X -> create tombstone
  - Replicate tombstone
  - Even with old write, tombstone remains
*/

/*
11.2.20: Merkle Tree
Hash tree for efficient sync
Identify differing subtrees
Sync only differences

Test Cases:
  - 1000 items, only 1 differs
  - Merkle hash identifies subtree
  - Sync only that subtree
*/

/*
11.2.21: Write-Ahead Logging
Log write before applying
Replay log on recovery
Durability guarantee

Test Cases:
  - Write log entry
  - Write succeeds (durability guaranteed)
  - On recovery, replay log
*/

/*
11.2.22: Read Repair
On read, detect stale replica
Send latest value to stale replica
Repair in background

Test Cases:
  - Read X from replica
  - Detect replica is old
  - Update replica with latest X
  - Consistency increases over time
*/

/*
11.2.23: Anti-Entropy
Background process to sync replicas
Compare and merge
Ensure eventual consistency

Test Cases:
  - Periodic (every 1 hour)
  - Compare replicas
  - Sync any differences
  - Ensures no permanent divergence
*/

/*
11.2.24: Gossip Protocol
Node sends state to random node
Receiving node updates and forwards
Rapid propagation

Test Cases:
  - 100 nodes
  - Update at 1 node
  - Gossip spreads to all in O(log N) rounds
  - No central coordinator needed
*/

/*
11.2.25: Sniffing
Listen to gossip between other nodes
Learn state without direct message
Reduce load on target

Test Cases:
  - Node1 and Node2 gossip
  - Node3 listens to gossip
  - Node3 learns about updates
  - No message directly to Node3
*/

/*
11.2.26: Epidemic Dissemination
Information spreads like epidemic
Exponential propagation
Guaranteed delivery with high probability

Test Cases:
  - Each node tells 3 others
  - In round 1: 4 nodes know
  - Round 2: 13 nodes know
  - Very fast spread
*/

/*
11.2.27: Replication Pipeline
Primary -> Backup1 -> Backup2
Sequential replication
Reduce primary load

Test Cases:
  - Write primary
  - Primary -> Backup1
  - Backup1 -> Backup2
  - All 3 eventually consistent
*/

/*
11.2.28: Parallel Replication
Primary -> Backup1, Backup2, Backup3
All backups in parallel
Reduce replication latency

Test Cases:
  - Write primary
  - Primary simultaneously -> all 3 backups
  - All acknowledge to primary
  - Faster than sequential
*/

/*
11.2.29: Replication Failure Handling
Backup dies during replication
Primary continues
Promote different backup
Re-replicate as needed

Test Cases:
  - Primary writes to 2 backups
  - Backup1 dies mid-replication
  - Primary continues with Backup2
  - Later sync Backup1 when back
*/

/*
11.2.30: Rebalancing
Replica nodes added/removed
Rebalance data distribution
No data loss

Test Cases:
  - 3-node cluster
  - Add 4th node
  - Data rebalance: each node 25%
  - No data loss
*/

/*
11.2.31: Replica Placement
Distribute across fault domains
Not all in same datacenter
Survive datacenter failure

Test Cases:
  - 3 replicas
  - Replica1: DC1
  - Replica2: DC2
  - Replica3: DC3
  - Survive 1 DC failure
*/

/*
11.2.32: Replica Health Check
Monitor replica health
Mark down if unhealthy
Redirect reads from unhealthy

Test Cases:
  - Replica becomes slow
  - Health check detects
  - Route reads away
  - Don't use unhealthy replica
*/

/*
11.2.33: Replica Promotion
Promote backup to primary
Old primary may rejoin as backup
Promote when primary dies

Test Cases:
  - Primary dies
  - Backup elected new primary
  - Old primary recovers
  - Rejoins as backup to new primary
*/

/*
11.2.34: Replica Down Handling
One replica down
Others continue
No data loss if replication factor > 1

Test Cases:
  - Replica down
  - Read from others
  - Write to others
  - Continue normally
*/

/*
11.2.35: Replica Synchronization
Sync replica with latest state
Batch updates
Minimize bandwidth

Test Cases:
  - Replica lags by 1000 items
  - Sync: batch 100 item batches
  - 10 batches to catch up
  - Eventually in sync
*/

