// CONSENSUS & SHARDING (30 problems)
// Problems 11.3.1 - 11.3.30

/*
11.3.1: Leader Election
Elect leader among nodes
Coordinate distributed decisions
kmeth leader failure

Test Cases:
  - 5 nodes, no leader
  - Election process
  - 1 node becomes leader
  - Others follow leader
*/

/*
11.3.2: Raft Consensus Part 1
Leader selected by nodes
Leader proposes decisions
Nodes acknowledge

Test Cases:
  - Leader sends proposal
  - Majority acknowledge
  - Proposal committed
*/

/*
11.3.3: Raft Term Numbers
Each term has one leader
Terms monotonically increase
Used to detect stale leaders

Test Cases:
  - Node sees term 5
  - Old request from term 3
  - Reject term 3 (stale)
*/

/*
11.3.4: Raft Log Replication
Leader replicates log entries
Followers append to log
Matching prefixes guarantee

Test Cases:
  - Leader has [A, B, C]
  - Followers replicate
  - Eventually all have [A, B, C]
*/

/*
11.3.5: Raft Safety
Once committed, never revert
Selection guarantees leader has all committed entries
Ensure consistency

Test Cases:
  - Entry committed
  - Leader fails
  - New leader guarantees to have committed entry
*/

/*
11.3.6: Paxos Consensus
Proposers, acceptors, learners
Two phases: prepare and accept
Liveness and safety

Test Cases:
  - Proposer p1 proposes value V1
  - Proposer p2 proposes value V2
  - Protocol ensures agreement on one value
*/

/*
11.3.7: Byzantine Fault Tolerance
Tolerate malicious nodes
Nodes may lie or act arbitrarily
Need 3f + 1 nodes to tolerate f Byzantine nodes

Test Cases:
  - 7 nodes, tolerate 2 Byzantine
  - 2 nodes lie
  - Remaining 5 reach consensus
*/

/*
11.3.8: Quorum-based Consensus
Quorum of nodes must agree
Simple majority or custom quorum
Ensure one group active

Test Cases:
  - 5 nodes, quorum = 3
  - If 3 agree, decision made
  - If split 3-2, majority 3 decides
*/

/*
11.3.9: Distributed Lock (2-phase)
Request lock from majority
Hold lock with lease
Release after use

Test Cases:
  - Acquire lock -> lock holder
  - Attempt lock by other -> wait
  - Release lock -> other gets lock
*/

/*
11.3.10: Distributed Transactions
2-phase commit
Coordinator, participants
Ensure all-or-nothing

Test Cases:
  - Coordinator says "commit"
  - All participants commit
  - Or all rollback
*/

/*
11.3.11: Prepare Phase
Coordinator asks: "can you commit?"
Participants respond: "yes" or "no"
If any says "no", whole transaction aborts

Test Cases:
  - 3 participants
  - All say "yes"
  - Proceed to commit
  - 1 says "no"
  - Abort all
*/

/*
11.3.12: Commit Phase
Coordinator says "commit"
All participants commit
Acknowledge to coordinator

Test Cases:
  - All prepared
  - Commit phase
  - All commit
  - Transaction complete
*/

/*
11.3.13: Rollback on Failure
If any participant fails
Rollback all others
Return to prior state

Test Cases:
  - Prepare succeeds on all
  - Commit fails on one
  - Rollback all others
  - No partial commits
*/

/*
11.3.14: Coordinator Failure
Coordinator crashes during transaction
Participants must detect
Abort transaction

Test Cases:
  - Coordinator dies after prepare
  - Before commit
  - Participants timeout
  - Abort transaction
*/

/*
11.3.15: Sharding Strategy
Partition data by key
Key -> shard mapping
Each shard independent

Test Cases:
  - Shard by user_id % 10
  - UserID 5 -> Shard 5
  - UserID 15 -> Shard 5
  - Consistent mapping
*/

/*
11.3.16: Range-based Sharding
Shard by key range
Keys 1-100 -> Shard1
Keys 101-200 -> Shard2
Hot spots if ranges uneven

Test Cases:
  - Shard by last name
  - A-F -> Shard1
  - G-M -> Shard2
  - N-Z -> Shard3
*/

/*
11.3.17: Hash-based Sharding
Hash key, mod by shard count
Uniform distribution
Consistent hashing for rebalancing

Test Cases:
  - hash(key) % 10 = shard
  - hash("user5") % 10 = 3
  - hash("user17") % 10 = 7
  - Usually well distributed
*/

/*
11.3.18: Consistent Hashing
Virtual nodes for load balancing
Minimal rebalancing on node add/remove
Better distribution than simple hashing

Test Cases:
  - Add node: only ~1/N keys move
  - Remove node: keys redistributed
  - Smooth rebalancing
*/

/*
11.3.19: Shard Splitting
Shard too large
Split into two smaller shards
Rebalance keys

Test Cases:
  - Shard 5 has 1M keys
  - Split to Shard 5a and 5b
  - Each ~500K keys
  - Both smaller and manageable
*/

/*
11.3.20: Shard Merge
Multiple small shards
Merge into one larger
Reduce overhead

Test Cases:
  - Shard 1: 100K keys
  - Shard 2: 100K keys
  - Merge to Shard 1+2: 200K keys
  - One shard to manage
*/

/*
11.3.21: Shard Migration
Move shard from node1 to node2
Data transfer
No service disruption

Test Cases:
  - Shard 5 on Node1
  - Migrate to Node2
  - During migration: handle requests
  - After migration: serve from Node2
*/

/*
11.3.22: Shard Replication
Replicate each shard
Shard A: primary on Node1, backup on Node2
Survive node failure

Test Cases:
  - Shard primary dies
  - Backup promoted
  - No data loss
*/

/*
11.3.23: Shard Routing
Route request to correct shard
Key determines shard
Route to primary or backup

Test Cases:
  - Request for key "user5"
  - Shard = hash(key) % 10 = 5
  - Route to Shard5's primary
*/

/*
11.3.24: Shard Rebalancing
Add/remove shard nodes
Redistribute keys
Maintain balance

Test Cases:
  - 3 nodes, each 33% data
  - Add 4th node
  - Rebalance: each 25% data
  - Some keys move
*/

/*
11.3.25: Shard Health Check
Monitor shard health
Mark unhealthy shards
Route away from unhealthy

Test Cases:
  - Shard slow
  - Health check detects
  - Route to healthy backup
*/

/*
11.3.26: Shard Failover
Shard primary dies
Backup promoted automatically
Clients redirect to new primary

Test Cases:
  - Node1 (primary) dies
  - Node2 (backup) elected primary
  - Clients route to Node2
*/

/*
11.3.27: Cross-Shard Queries
Query spans multiple shards
Fan-out to all shards
Aggregate results

Test Cases:
  - Query all users
  - Spans all shards
  - Query each shard
  - Combine results
*/

/*
11.3.28: Cross-Shard Transactions
Transaction affects multiple shards
Use 2-phase commit
Ensure consistency

Test Cases:
  - Transfer from Shard1 to Shard2
  - Shard1: debit
  - Shard2: credit
  - Both succeed or both fail
*/

/*
11.3.29: Shard Topology
Map of which nodes own which shards
Metadata service tracks topology
Clients use topology for routing

Test Cases:
  - Node1: Shards 1, 2, 3
  - Node2: Shards 4, 5, 6
  - Clients query topology
  - Route requests correctly
*/

/*
11.3.30: Shard Metadata
Version metadata when topology changes
Clients detect stale topology
Re-fetch metadata

Test Cases:
  - Version 1: Node1 has Shard1
  - Migration to Node2
  - Version 2: Node2 has Shard1
  - Client sees version mismatch
  - Re-fetch metadata
*/

